1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,085
Why do atoms form covalent bonds? It all comes down to electrons, and to stability.

2
00:00:06,035 --> 00:00:13,535
An atom is most stable when its outer shell of electrons is full. Hydrogen has one electron, but its shell has room for two.

3
00:00:13,885 --> 00:00:17,194
Chlorine has seven outer electrons, but its shell holds eight.

4
00:00:17,544 --> 00:00:21,805
Each of these atoms is one electron short of a full, stable outer shell.

5
00:00:22,755 --> 00:00:26,935
When two hydrogen atoms come close together, something useful can happen.

6
00:00:27,284 --> 00:00:31,696
Their outer shells overlap, so electrons can be shared between both atoms.

7
00:00:32,046 --> 00:00:36,748
The two electrons form a shared pair of electrons that sits between the two nuclei.

8
00:00:37,098 --> 00:00:42,659
Now each hydrogen atom counts two electrons in its outer shell. That shared pair is a covalent bond.

9
00:00:43,609 --> 00:00:47,383
Hydrogen gas is two hydrogen atoms joined by one shared pair.

10
00:00:47,733 --> 00:00:51,529
In water, oxygen shares one pair with each of two hydrogen atoms.

11
00:00:51,879 --> 00:00:56,848
And chlorine gas forms when two chlorine atoms share a pair, so both reach eight.

12
00:00:57,798 --> 00:01:01,664
To recap: atoms are most stable when their outer shell is full.

13
00:01:02,014 --> 00:01:05,880
Instead of giving electrons away, non-metal atoms can share them.

14
00:01:06,230 --> 00:01:11,165
Each shared pair of electrons is a covalent bond, and it makes both atoms more stable.
